  <!-- Real operational note instead of generic setup tips -->
  <h2>The "Unlimited" Fine Print Saily Doesn't Put on the Homepage</h2>
  <p>
    Saily's own fair-use policy caps "unlimited" plans at full speed for the first <strong>5GB in a rolling 24-hour window in most destinations (3GB in some)</strong>, then throttles to 1Mbps until the local-midnight reset. That's a genuinely usable amount for normal phone use (maps, messaging, video calls, some streaming), but it's worth knowing before you assume "unlimited" means no ceiling at all, especially if several people are sharing one plan's hotspot.
  </p>
  <p>
    There's also a subscription tier most competitors don't have an equivalent of: <strong>Saily Ultra</strong>, at $59.99/month, bundles 30GB of high-speed data across 121+ destinations (then unlimited at 1Mbps), a full NordVPN/NordPass/NordLayer/Incogni subscription bundle, 8% cashback in Saily credit, and one airport lounge pass plus one fast-track security pass per month that roll over for up to three months if unused. It's a real product for someone who travels constantly and would otherwise be paying for the VPN suite anyway. It's a bad deal for an occasional traveler who just needs data for one trip.
  </p>
  <p>
    One limitation worth flagging directly: Saily is among the eSIM providers whose websites and apps were blocked in Turkey by the country's telecom regulator (BTK) starting mid-2025, alongside Airalo, Holafly, and several others. An eSIM bought and installed <em>before</em> you land still works once you're there: the block affects buying or topping up while physically in Turkey, not eSIMs already active on your phone. Worth planning around if Turkey is on the itinerary.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply beyond Saily specifically, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

